  "worked_solution": "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+3}{{x}^{2}+x-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+3}{(x+3)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+3}}{\\cancel{(x+3)}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$",
